  How much does each side contribute to a peak height?

   sigma  shortest side   longest side    K(short)     K(long)  ratio to K(0)=1
    0.22         0.7976         1.9324    1.40e-03    1.76e-17          1.8e-17
    0.26         0.7976         1.9324    9.05e-03    1.01e-12          1.0e-12
    0.30         0.7976         1.9324    2.92e-02    9.78e-10          9.8e-10
    0.34         0.7976         1.9324    6.38e-02    9.67e-08          9.7e-08

  So the longest side changes the peak heights in the 12th to 30th decimal
  place, while the heights themselves are of order 1. Recovering it needs
  more precision than double arithmetic carries. The map is invertible in
  principle and useless as an inversion in practice.

  What survives is the ORDERING. Which of h_1, h_2, h_3 is largest is
  decided by differences of order 1e-2, comfortably above the noise, and
  it matched the ordering of the opposite sides at 858 of 858 instants.

  That is the point worth keeping: the braid is built from the ordering and
  not from the values, so it uses exactly the well conditioned part of the
  diagram and discards the part that is not recoverable.
